# Retired launchd jobs (#432 — Option C; issue #431 decision).
#
# hub-state-check (repo drift) + skill-lint-oracle (skill-lint drift lock)
# cannot run under launchd: macOS TCC blocks launchd-spawned processes from
# reading ~/Documents (EPERM for bash, git, node AND python — #427 probe),
# and both jobs inherently read the repos there. Their work moved to
# extensions/session-checks.ts, which runs them age-gated (hub >6h, oracle
# >24h) from pi's session_start — an interactive, TCC-approved process tree.
#
# install-launchd.sh unloads + removes jobs listed here on machines that
# installed them pre-retirement. A label here RETIRES the job even if a *.plist
# template for it still exists (the manifest wins on a machine — #2908): the
# job is not scheduled, and an installed copy is unloaded and removed. Retiring
# a job is therefore an operator action on ONE file, not a repo change that also
# has to delete a template.
#
# ⛔ BUT THE TEMPLATE SHOULD STILL GO, and the repo's declaration gate says so:
# `scripts/fleet-schedule.py --check` exits 1 for any label listed here that
# still has a template. That is the AUTHORING check (both statements live in
# the repo, so that is where the contradiction is caught) and it is the only
# surface that refuses it — `install-launchd.sh` warns (and still unloads +
# removes), and `fleet-schedule.py --install/--start/--verify/--status` report
# the label as a named SKIP; both honour the manifest instead, because a single
# stale line here must never disarm a whole cell's scheduled layer (no
# reaping, no liveness detection, no canary — measured on cells 3 and 4,
# 2026-10-27). On macOS the schedule verbs are the installer's, and it names the
# same state: `install-launchd.sh --status` prints
# `RETIRED (manifest wins — template present; remove the template in the repo)`
# rather than `NOT INSTALLED`, so `fleet-schedule.py --verify` (which delegates
# to that verb) certifies the layer instead of reporting it broken — a retired
# job is deliberately never installed, and an absence is not a failure. A copy
# still installed after retirement is reported `DRIFTED`, so closing that false
# FAIL does not launder a stale job into a clean status: re-run the installer
# and it is unloaded + removed.
#
# Corruption-canary + provider-latency-tripwire REMAIN launchd jobs: the
# canary watches ~/swarm (unprotected), the tripwire touches no protected
# path — both run fine under launchd.
com.eldato.hub-state-check
com.eldato.skill-lint-oracle
#
# ── CONTROLLER WATCHDOG (#2303) — RETIRED, NOT MERELY SUPERSEDED ──────────────────────────────────
# com.eldato.orchestrator-watchdog was the Mac's HAND-PROVISIONED controller watchdog (`StartInterval`
# 20 → /Users/danielospina/orchestrator/watchdog.sh, absolute home hardcoded; docs/ops/
# ACTIVATION-MATRIX.md row 8 recorded it as "installed by no surface in this repo"). Its replacement
# SHIPS now: templates/launchd/com.eldato.controller-watchdog.plist (`StartInterval` 300) →
# scripts/orchestrator-harness/watchdog-loop.sh, which is the ship-able copy of the same intent and
# the one the fleet's inventory can start, stop and read.
#
# ⛔ THIS ENTRY IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT TIDINESS: the two are NOT a redundant pair. The old job ran
# `watchdog.sh` (check → load → log → exit, IDEMPOTENT); the new one takes a NON-idempotent
# `launchctl kickstart -k`. Two loaded watchdogs would therefore be two MUTATING actors restarting one
# controller, and the "both invoke the same idempotent script, so they cannot disagree" argument that
# docs/ops/fleet-liveness.md used to justify the pair would no longer hold. Leaving both installed is
# exactly the state this file exists to prevent.
#
# Nothing is INSTALLED by the change that adds this entry: retiring a job is the installer's job (the
# `retire_pass` in install-launchd.sh unloads + removes it), and running the installer is a separate,
# deliberate step.
com.eldato.orchestrator-watchdog

# ── THE BLIND HEARTBEAT (#1867) — RETIRED, and this entry IS the remediation ──────────────────────
# `com.tortoise.heartbeat-loop` is the launchd job that ran scripts/heartbeat-loop.sh on the Mac. NO
# template in this directory ever named it (`pi-bootstrap/tests/test-setup-no-nesting.sh` records the
# same fact, and `scripts/lane-status.py:41` names the label in a comment about its empty
# environment), so it was installed by hand — which is exactly why the UNMANAGED instrument exists
# and exactly why it could not be removed by any command in this repo: `--uninstall` iterates
# TEMPLATES, and no template describes a hand-installed job.
#
# ⛔ WHY IT MUST GO RATHER THAN BE KEPT: the cmux socket is `cmuxOnly`, which gates on launch-chain
# ATTRIBUTION, and a launchd job never has it. The job ticks, logs `Access denied - only processes
# started inside cmux can connect`, and delivers NOTHING — measured 1,695 degraded reads, 210
# refusals, 0 nudges, with a log that reads as activity. The launch path is
# `scripts/heartbeat-launch.sh start` from a cmux-attributed shell (#1867), and
# `templates/launchd/LAUNCHD-INCOMPATIBLE` now refuses to install any template that would name the
# loop. This entry is the other half: it REMOVES the instance already on the box.
#
# Nothing is INSTALLED by adding this entry. `retire_pass` unloads + removes it on the next install
# (and on `--uninstall`), so the box stops ticking a blind job without a human hand-deleting a plist.
com.tortoise.heartbeat-loop
